January 3

[LINK: (Tseax Cone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tseax_Cone]  is a small  [LINK: (volcano) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcano]  in the  [LINK: (Nass Ranges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nass_Ranges]  of the  [LINK: (Hazelton Mountains) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazelton_Mountains]  in northwestern  [LINK: (British Columbia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Columbia] , Canada. It has an  [LINK: (elevation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elevation]  of 609 metres (1,998 feet) and lies within an east–west valley through which a  [LINK: (tributary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tributary]  of the  [LINK: (Tseax River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ksi_Sii_Aks]  flows. The volcano consists of two nested structures and was the source of four  [LINK: (lava) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lava]  flows that descended into neighbouring valleys. A  [LINK: (secondary eruptive centre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parasitic_cone]  lies just north of Tseax Cone on the opposite side of  [LINK: (Melita Lake) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melita_Lake] . It probably formed simultaneously with Tseax Cone; both were formed by volcanic activity sometime in the last 800 years. The exact timing of volcanism at Tseax Cone has been a subject of controversy due to there being no direct written accounts. There is also controversy over whether the volcano was formed during one or more distinct episodes of eruptive activity. The single eruptive episode hypothesis has been proposed by researchers as early as 1923 whereas a multi-eruption hypothesis was proposed in 1978. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tseax_Cone] )

