January 4

[LINK: (The remains of Richard III) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exhumation_and_reburial_of_Richard_III_of_England] , the last  [LINK: (English king killed in battle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_monarchs_of_the_British_Isles_by_cause_of_death#Killed]  and last king of the  [LINK: (House of York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_York] , were discovered within the site of the former  [LINK: (Greyfriars Friary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greyfriars,_Leicester]  in  [LINK: (Leicester) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leicester] , England, in September 2012.  [LINK: (Richard III) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_III_of_England] , the final ruler of the  [LINK: (Plantagenet dynasty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Plantagenet] , was killed on 22 August 1485 in the  [LINK: (Battle of Bosworth Field) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bosworth_Field] . His body was taken to  [LINK: (Greyfriars) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greyfriars,_Leicester] , where it was buried in a crude grave in the friary church. Following the friary's  [LINK: (dissolution in 1538) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dissolution_of_the_monasteries]  and subsequent demolition, Richard's tomb was lost. A search for Richard's body began in August 2012 and that September an  [LINK: (archaeological excavation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaeological_excavation]  took place at the site of the friary. A skeleton (pictured) was discovered of a man with a  [LINK: (spinal deformity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scoliosis]  and severe head injuries. Following extensive anthropological and genetic testing, the remains were identified as those of Richard.  [LINK: (Leicester Cathedral) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leicester_Cathedral]  was chosen as the site of Richard's reburial. His reinterment took place on 26 March 2015, during a televised memorial service. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exhumation_and_reburial_of_Richard_III_of_England] )

