January 8

The  [LINK: (Yamato-class battleships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamato-class_battleship]  were two battleships of the  [LINK: (Imperial Japanese Navy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_Japanese_Navy] ,  [LINK: (Yamato) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_battleship_Yamato]  (pictured) and  [LINK: (Musashi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_battleship_Musashi] ,   [LINK: (laid down) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keel_laying]  leading up to the Second World War and completed as designed. A third hull was converted to the aircraft carrier  [LINK: (Shinano) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Shinano]  during construction. Displacing nearly 72,000 long tons (73,000 t), the completed battleships were the heaviest ever constructed. The class carried the largest  [LINK: (naval artillery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_artillery]  ever fitted to a warship, nine  [LINK: (460 mm (18.1 in) naval guns) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/46_cm/45_Type_94_naval_gun] , capable of firing 1,460 kg (3,220 lb) shells over 42 km (26 mi). Because of the threat of U.S. submarines and aircraft carriers, Yamato and Musashi spent the majority of their careers in naval bases. All three ships were sunk by the  [LINK: (U.S. Navy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Navy] : Musashi by air strikes while participating in the  [LINK: (Battle of Leyte Gulf) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Leyte_Gulf]  in October 1944, Shinano after being torpedoed by the submarine  [LINK: (USS Archerfish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Archerfish_(SS-311)]  in November 1944, and Yamato by air strikes while en route to  [LINK: (Okinawa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okinawa_Island]  in April 1945. ( [LINK: (This article) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamato-class_battleship]  is part of a  [LINK: (featured topic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Featured_topics] :  [LINK: (Yamato-class battleships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Featured_topics/Yamato-class_battleships] .)

