January 12

[LINK: (Elizabeth Alkin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Alkin]  (c. 1600 – c. 1655) was a publisher, nurse and spy for the  [LINK: (Parliamentarian forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roundhead]  during the  [LINK: (English Civil War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Civil_War]  (1642–1651). She was also commonly known as Parliamentary Joan, one of many derogatory names she was called by  [LINK: (royalist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cavalier]  sympathisers. Little is known about Alkin's early life. Her husband was arrested and hanged in 1643 by the royalists during the English Civil War for spying for the Parliamentarians; Alkin continued his work, spying in  [LINK: (Oxford) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford] —the royalist wartime capital—even during  [LINK: (the city's siege) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Oxford] . By 1648 Alkin was involved in selling and then publishing Parliamentary  [LINK: (newsbooks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newsbook]  (example pictured)—the forerunners of newspapers. She used her role as a vendor to track down and report several publishers of royalist material. After the civil war, Alkin nursed casualties of the  [LINK: (First Anglo-Dutch War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Anglo-Dutch_War] , initially in  [LINK: (Portsmouth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portsmouth] , then  [LINK: (Harwich) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harwich]  and  [LINK: (Ipswich) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipswich] . With her health failing she returned to London. It is presumed she died shortly afterwards, possibly over the 1655 Christmas period. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Alkin] )

