January 14

[LINK: (The Snow Queen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Snow_Queen_(Kernaghan_novel)]  is a 2000  [LINK: (young-adult) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Young_adult_literature]  fantasy novel by the Canadian writer  [LINK: (Eileen Kernaghan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eileen_Kernaghan] . It follows Gerda, a young Danish woman who sets out to rescue her childhood friend Kai from Madame Aurore, a magician known as the Snow Queen. She is joined on her journey by Ritva, a young  [LINK: (Sámi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%A1mi_people]  woman born to a  [LINK: (shamaness) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamanism]  and a robber. The novel is based on  [LINK: (Hans Christian Andersen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Christian_Andersen] 's fairy tale " [LINK: (The Snow Queen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Snow_Queen] " (1844), but incorporates elements of Scandinavian shamanism and influences from the  [LINK: (Kalevala) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalevala]  (1835), a compilation of  [LINK: (Finnish mythology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnish_mythology]  and  [LINK: (epic poetry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_poetry] . It also explores  [LINK: (feminist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feminism]  and matriarchal themes, reinterpreting several plot elements from Andersen's original with contemporary shifts against the background of the  [LINK: (imaginative geography) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imagined_geographies]  of Arctic landscapes. The Snow Queen was published by  [LINK: (Thistledown Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thistledown_Press]  and received positive reviews. It received the  [LINK: (Aurora Award for Best Novel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora_Award_for_Best_Novel]  in 2001 and was considered for two other accolades. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Snow_Queen_(Kernaghan_novel)] )

