January 15

[LINK: (Louis Abramson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Abramson]  (August 1, 1887 – January 15, 1985) was an  [LINK: (American architect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_American_architects]  who practiced mostly in New York City, specializing in hospitals, nursing homes, and restaurants. He is best known for designing the  [LINK: (Daughters of Jacob Geriatric Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Home_of_the_Daughters_of_Jacob]  (pictured). Early in his career, he designed several Jewish Centers, a new type of building which filled the religious, cultural, educational, and often fitness needs of the community in a single structure. Abramson had little formal schooling in architecture; he took courses at  [LINK: (Cooper Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooper_Union] , the  [LINK: (Mechanics Institute) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Society_of_Mechanics_and_Tradesmen_of_the_City_of_New_York] , and  [LINK: (Columbia University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_University]  but did not complete a degree. Most of his training was on-the-job in junior positions at well-known New York City architecture firms, after which he started his own firm. He employed a variety of styles, including  [LINK: (Neo-Renaissance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renaissance_Revival_architecture] ,  [LINK: (Moorish Revival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moorish_Revival_architecture] ,  [LINK: (Neo-Classical) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoclassical_architecture] ,  [LINK: (Tudor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tudor_architecture] ,  [LINK: (Art Deco) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Deco] , and  [LINK: (Art Moderne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Streamline_Moderne] . Several of his buildings are listed on the  [LINK: (National Register of Historic Places) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Register_of_Historic_Places] . ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Abramson] )

