January 17

[LINK: (Frank Hague) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Hague]  (January 17, 1876 – January 1, 1956) was an American politician who served as  [LINK: (the mayor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayor_of_Jersey_City,_New_Jersey]  of  [LINK: (Jersey City, New Jersey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey_City,_New_Jersey] , from 1917 to 1947. Hague ran a  [LINK: (political machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_machine]  that dominated politics in  [LINK: (Hudson County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_County,_New_Jersey]  and often in the entire state of New Jersey. Born into poverty in Jersey City, Hague first gained office at age 20 and rose through the political ranks. During his 30 years as mayor, Hague's influence reached the national level. His ability to gain huge majorities in Hudson County for the  [LINK: (Democrats) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)]  helped win the state in elections for governor and for president, and his machine dispensed jobs and aid in exchange for votes. He wielded his greatest power under  [LINK: (Franklin D. Roosevelt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_D._Roosevelt] , controlling funding for  [LINK: (New Deal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Deal]  projects. Among these were the  [LINK: (Jersey City Medical Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey_City_Medical_Center]  and  [LINK: (Roosevelt Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roosevelt_Stadium] . By the 1940s, Hague spent much time vacationing, and other ethnic groups challenged the  [LINK: (Irish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_Americans]  dominance in the city. He resigned in 1947 in favor of his nephew,  [LINK: (Frank Hague Eggers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_H._Eggers] . ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Hague] )

