January 19

[LINK: (King Edward III of England led a campaign) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_III%27s_Breton_campaign]  in the  [LINK: (Duchy of Brittany) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Brittany]  in 1342 and 1343.  [LINK: (England) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_England] , at war with  [LINK: (France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_France]  since 1337 in the  [LINK: (Hundred Years' War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hundred_Years%27_War] , had sided with  [LINK: (John of Montfort) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Montfort] 's faction in the  [LINK: (Breton Civil War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_Breton_Succession]  soon after it broke out in 1341. An English fleet broke the  [LINK: (blockade of Brest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Brest_(1342)]  on 18 August, and on 30 September a numerically inferior English army inflicted a heavy defeat on the French at the  [LINK: (battle of Morlaix) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Morlaix] . After logistical difficulties,  [LINK: (Edward) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_III]  abandoned his  [LINK: (siege train) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Train_(military)]  and landed in Brittany on 26 October after a storm-wracked three-week passage. The English then advanced on the major Breton town of  [LINK: (Vannes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vannes] . The French garrison repelled an immediate assault and the English  [LINK: (began a siege) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sieges_of_Vannes_(1342)] . English raiding parties devastated large parts of eastern Brittany, but attempts to reinforce or supply Edward from England failed.  [LINK: (Philip VI of France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_VI_of_France]  moved his court to Brittany and entered into negotiations with Edward; they rapidly agreed the  [LINK: (Truce of Malestroit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truce_of_Malestroit] , which is widely seen as favouring the English. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_III%27s_Breton_campaign] )

