January 20

The  [LINK: (volcano rabbit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcano_rabbit]  (Romerolagus diazi) is a small species of  [LINK: (rabbit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit]  that lives in  [LINK: (pine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine]  and  [LINK: (alder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alder]  forests on several  [LINK: (volcanic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcano]  slopes in Mexico. It is the  [LINK: (sole member) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monotypic_taxon]  of the  [LINK: (genus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genus]  Romerolagus and considered the most  [LINK: (primitive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_(phylogenetics)]  species among the rabbits and hares. One of the smallest rabbits in the world, it has an adult weight ranging from 390 to 600 g (14 to 21 oz) and a total body length of 23 to 31 cm (9.1 to 12.2 in). Dense, short, yellowish brown fur covers its body, helping it to blend in with volcanic soil. It has short, rounded ears, and its short tail becomes hidden in adulthood. The volcano rabbit has a highly restricted habitat, being found only on the slopes of four volcanoes to the southeast of  [LINK: (Mexico City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico_City] . Human activities, including urban and agricultural expansion, have caused its habitat to become more  [LINK: (fragmented) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitat_fragmentation] , and hunting, livestock grazing, and fires threaten rabbit populations even inside  [LINK: (protected areas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protected_area] . ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcano_rabbit] )

