January 24

[LINK: (Musidora: The Bather 'At the Doubtful Breeze Alarmed') -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musidora:_The_Bather_%27At_the_Doubtful_Breeze_Alarmed%27] , also known as The Bather, is a name given to four nearly identical  [LINK: (oil paintings) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oil_painting]  on canvas by English artist  [LINK: (William Etty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Etty] . The paintings illustrate a scene from  [LINK: (James Thomson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Thomson_(poet,_born_1700)] 's 1727 poem  [LINK: (Summer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seasons_(Thomson)]  in which a young man accidentally sees a young woman bathing naked and is torn between his desire to look and his knowledge that he ought to look away. The scene was popular with English artists as it was one of the few legitimate pretexts to paint  [LINK: (nudes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nude_(art)]  at a time when the display and distribution of nude imagery was suppressed. Musidora was extremely well received when first exhibited and considered one of the finest works by an English artist. Etty died in 1849 and his work rapidly went out of fashion. At that time, the topic of Musidora itself became a cliche, and from the 1870s Thomson's writings faded into obscurity. Etty's Musidora is likely to have influenced  [LINK: (The Knight Errant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Knight_Errant_(painting)]  by  [LINK: (John Everett Millais) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Everett_Millais] , but otherwise has had little effect. ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musidora:_The_Bather_%27At_the_Doubtful_Breeze_Alarmed%27] )

