January 28

[LINK: (The Ladies' Journal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ladies%27_Journal]  was the longest-lasting and widest-circulating Chinese  [LINK: (women's magazine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_women%27s_magazines]  during the  [LINK: (Republican period) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China_(1912%E2%80%931949)] . Published from 1915 to 1931 by the  [LINK: (Commercial Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commercial_Press] , it initially focused on domestic issues and short stories of the  [LINK: (Mandarin Ducks and Butterflies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandarin_Ducks_and_Butterflies]  school of romantic fiction. Following criticism over the journal's stances, Shen Yanbing ( [LINK: (Mao Dun) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_Dun] ) was hired as editor in 1920. Shen left later that year, and Zhang Xichen replaced him as editor. While lacking prior interest in women's issues, he became a dedicated liberal feminist and recruited like-minded writers such as his assistant  [LINK: (Zhou Jianren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhou_Jianren] . A controversial 1925 special issue on the "new sexual morality" alongside political disagreements with the Commercial Press led to their removal as editors. The journal returned to a domestic focus. Already struggling due to decreased advertiser investment, the journal was cancelled after its headquarters were destroyed in  [LINK: (a month-long battle between Chinese and Japanese forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_28_incident] . ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ladies%27_Journal] )

