January 29

[LINK: (Opifex fuscus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opifex_fuscus]  is a species of  [LINK: (mosquito) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosquito]  that is  [LINK: (endemic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endemism]  to New Zealand. They occur along the coast, where their  [LINK: (larvae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larva]  live in rock pools within the  [LINK: (spray zone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supralittoral_zone] . To survive in this habitat, the larvae are able to tolerate a wide range of water  [LINK: (salt concentrations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salinity] . The larvae feed on  [LINK: (algae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algae]  and decomposing matter. They also have  [LINK: (mouthparts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insect_mouthparts]  that specialise towards either  [LINK: (filter feeding) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filter_feeder]  or  [LINK: (grazing) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grazing] , depending on what food sources are available to them. As adults the females feed on the blood of birds and humans. They have an unusual mating system where the males wait on the surface of the rock pools and mate with female  [LINK: (pupae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pupa]  before they have matured into adults. They are widespread throughout the rocky coasts of New Zealand but have been displaced from the  [LINK: (Otago) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otago]  region by the introduced species  [LINK: (Aedes australis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aedes_australis] . The larvae are also known to be infected by the fungus  [LINK: (Coelomomyces psorophorae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coelomomyces_psorophorae] , which uses  [LINK: (copepods) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copepod]  as intermediate  [LINK: (hosts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Host_(biology)] . ( [LINK: (Full article...) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opifex_fuscus] )

